import {useEffect,useMemo,useState} from "react";

const RELEASE_AT=new Date("2026-10-01T00:00:00+05:30");

const Q=[
["Who is widely regarded as the “Father of the Computer”?",["John von Neumann","Charles Babbage","Herman Hollerith","Alan Turing"],1,"Correct: Charles Babbage. John von Neumann is associated with stored-program architecture; Herman Hollerith developed punched-card tabulation; Alan Turing is associated with the Turing Machine and major work in theoretical computer science/codebreaking."],
["Charles Babbage designed which machine that is considered a major precursor to modern computers?",["ENIAC","UNIVAC","Analytical Engine","Mark-I"],2,"Correct: Analytical Engine. ENIAC was an early electronic general-purpose computer; UNIVAC I was an early commercial computer; Harvard Mark I was an electromechanical computer associated with Howard Aiken."],
["The Difference Engine was primarily designed to:",["Perform logical operations","Perform differential calculations and produce mathematical tables","Store programs electronically","Process punched cards"],1,"Correct: differential calculations and mathematical tables. Babbage's Difference Engine was a mechanical calculator based on finite-difference methods. It was not an electronic stored-program machine and did not use Hollerith-style punched-card data processing."],
["Who developed the concept of the Analytical Engine?",["Charles Babbage","Howard Aiken","John Mauchly","John Presper Eckert"],0,"Correct: Charles Babbage. Howard Aiken is linked to Harvard Mark I; John Mauchly and J. Presper Eckert are linked to ENIAC. The Analytical Engine anticipated processor, memory, input and output concepts."],
["Augusta Ada King, Countess of Lovelace, is associated with which of the following?",["First electronic computer","First computer algorithm intended for a machine","First transistor","First microprocessor"],1,"Correct: first published algorithm intended for a machine, for Babbage's Analytical Engine. Ada Lovelace is widely regarded as the first computer programmer. The first transistor was developed at Bell Labs; Intel 4004 was an early commercial microprocessor."],
["Who developed the punched-card tabulating machine that later became associated with IBM's origins?",["Herman Hollerith","Blaise Pascal","Joseph Jacquard","Konrad Zuse"],0,"Correct: Herman Hollerith. Pascal developed Pascaline; Joseph Jacquard used punched cards to control weaving patterns in the Jacquard loom; Konrad Zuse built the Z-series programmable machines, including Z3."],
["Herman Hollerith's tabulating machine was famously used to process data from the:",["1880 UK Census","1890 US Census","1900 US Census","1891 UK Census"],1,"Correct: 1890 US Census. Hollerith's punched-card system greatly accelerated census data processing and became an important milestone in data processing."],
["Which inventor developed the Pascaline?",["Blaise Pascal","Gottfried Leibniz","Charles Babbage","George Boole"],0,"Correct: Blaise Pascal. Pascaline was a mechanical calculator. Leibniz developed the Stepped Reckoner; Babbage designed the Difference and Analytical Engines; George Boole developed Boolean algebra."],
["The Pascaline was primarily a:",["Mechanical calculating machine","Electronic computer","Programmable computer","Vacuum-tube computer"],0,"Correct: mechanical calculating machine. It used mechanical wheels/gears for arithmetic. It was centuries before electronic computers, so it did not use vacuum tubes or electronic programming."],
["Who developed the Stepped Reckoner, a mechanical calculator capable of multiplication and division?",["Joseph Jacquard","Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz","Herman Hollerith","Howard Aiken"],1,"Correct: Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz. The Stepped Reckoner was a mechanical calculator capable of addition, subtraction, multiplication and division. Jacquard is associated with punched-card weaving; Hollerith with tabulation; Aiken with Harvard Mark I."],
["Which technology was the principal electronic component of first-generation computers?",["Transistors","Integrated circuits","Vacuum tubes","Microprocessors"],2,"Correct: vacuum tubes. Transistors define the second generation, integrated circuits the third, and microprocessors/VLSI the fourth. Vacuum tubes made first-generation machines large, hot and power-hungry."],
["Which of the following is a characteristic of first-generation computers?",["Very small size and low heat generation","Use of machine language","Use of VLSI technology","Use of graphical user interfaces"],1,"Correct: machine language. First-generation computers were large, generated substantial heat and used vacuum tubes. VLSI is associated with later generations, while graphical user interfaces became common much later."],
["Which of the following is generally associated with the first generation of computers?",["ENIAC","IBM PC","Apple Macintosh","Intel 4004"],0,"Correct: ENIAC. ENIAC was a first-generation vacuum-tube computer. IBM PC and early Macintosh systems belong to the microprocessor era; Intel 4004 is a microprocessor from 1971."],
["ENIAC stands for:",["Electronic Numerical Integrator and Computer","Electronic Numerical Integrated Automatic Computer","Electrical Numerical Integrator and Calculator","Electronic Network Integrated Automatic Computer"],0,"Correct: Electronic Numerical Integrator and Computer. ENIAC was an early electronic general-purpose computer developed during the 1940s using vacuum tubes."],
["ENIAC was primarily developed by:",["Charles Babbage and Ada Lovelace","John Mauchly and J. Presper Eckert","Howard Aiken and Grace Hopper","John von Neumann and Alan Turing"],1,"Correct: John Mauchly and J. Presper Eckert. Babbage/Lovelace are associated with early mechanical computing; Aiken with Harvard Mark I; von Neumann with stored-program architecture."],
["Which of the following was a major limitation of ENIAC?",["It used transistors","It had no arithmetic capability","It used a large number of vacuum tubes and consumed enormous power","It could not perform calculations"],2,"Correct: large numbers of vacuum tubes and enormous power consumption. ENIAC could perform complex calculations; it did not use transistors. Vacuum tubes caused heat, size and reliability/maintenance issues."],
["EDVAC is particularly significant because it incorporated the concept of:",["Punched-card processing","Stored-program architecture","Cloud computing","Artificial intelligence"],1,"Correct: stored-program architecture. EDVAC is associated with storing instructions and data in memory. Cloud computing and modern AI are much later technologies; punched-card processing predates EDVAC."],
["The stored-program concept is strongly associated with:",["John von Neumann","Herman Hollerith","Blaise Pascal","Joseph Jacquard"],0,"Correct: John von Neumann. The von Neumann architecture uses memory to store both instructions and data. Hollerith is associated with punched-card tabulation, Pascal with Pascaline, and Jacquard with punched-card loom control."],
["Which generation introduced transistors as a replacement for vacuum tubes?",["First","Second","Third","Fourth"],1,"Correct: Second generation. First used vacuum tubes; second used transistors; third used ICs; fourth used microprocessors/VLSI."],
["Which of the following is most closely associated with second-generation computers?",["Machine language only","Transistors and assembly language","Integrated circuits and VLSI","Microprocessors"],1,"Correct: transistors and assembly language. Second-generation computers replaced vacuum tubes with transistors and saw wider use of assembly and high-level languages. ICs are third generation and microprocessors/VLSI fourth."],
["Which of the following was a major advantage of transistors over vacuum tubes?",["Higher power consumption","Larger physical size","Lower heat generation and greater reliability","Slower switching speed"],2,"Correct: lower heat generation and greater reliability. Transistors were smaller, faster, more durable and consumed less power than vacuum tubes. They enabled more compact and reliable computers."],
["Which generation of computers is associated with the introduction of Integrated Circuits (ICs)?",["First","Second","Third","Fourth"],2,"Correct: Third generation. ICs integrated multiple electronic components on a chip, improving speed, reliability, size and cost. First used vacuum tubes, second transistors, fourth microprocessors."],
["Which technology replaced individual transistors and other discrete components in many third-generation computers?",["Vacuum tubes","Integrated circuits","Magnetic drums","Mechanical gears"],1,"Correct: integrated circuits. An IC combines many electronic components on a semiconductor chip. Vacuum tubes and mechanical gears are older technologies; magnetic drums were an earlier memory technology."],
["Which programming languages became widely associated with third-generation computers?",["FORTRAN and COBOL","Machine language only","Assembly language only","HTML and CSS"],0,"Correct: FORTRAN and COBOL. FORTRAN means Formula Translation and became important for scientific/numerical computing; COBOL means Common Business-Oriented Language and was designed for business/data processing. HTML and CSS are web technologies, not generation-defining third-generation languages."],
["Who is popularly associated with the development of COBOL?",["Grace Hopper","Ada Lovelace","Kathleen Booth","Radia Perlman"],0,"Correct: Grace Hopper. She was a compiler pioneer and is strongly associated with COBOL. Ada Lovelace is associated with Babbage's Analytical Engine; Kathleen Booth worked on early assembly language; Radia Perlman is famous for networking/STP."],
["Which generation is associated with the development and widespread use of microprocessors?",["First","Second","Third","Fourth"],3,"Correct: Fourth generation. Microprocessors put central processing functions onto integrated circuits and enabled personal computers and much smaller systems. First/second/third correspond mainly to vacuum tubes/transistors/ICs."],
["The Intel 4004, released in 1971, is widely regarded as the first commercially available:",["Supercomputer","Microprocessor","Personal computer","Mainframe"],1,"Correct: microprocessor. Intel 4004 was a 4-bit microprocessor introduced in 1971. It was not itself a personal computer, mainframe or supercomputer."],
["The development of microprocessors was made possible primarily by advances in:",["Mechanical engineering","Integrated-circuit technology","Vacuum-tube technology","Punched-card technology"],1,"Correct: integrated-circuit technology. Increasing semiconductor integration led from ICs to VLSI and microprocessors. Mechanical gears, vacuum tubes and punched cards were important earlier technologies but did not enable modern microprocessors."],
["VLSI stands for:",["Very Large Scale Integration","Variable Large System Integration","Very Low Scale Integration","Virtual Large Scale Interface"],0,"Correct: Very Large Scale Integration. Other common integration levels include SSI (Small Scale Integration), MSI (Medium Scale Integration), LSI (Large Scale Integration) and ULSI (Ultra Large Scale Integration)."],
["VLSI technology is most closely associated with which generation?",["First","Second","Third","Fourth"],3,"Correct: Fourth generation. Fourth-generation computers are characterized by microprocessors and VLSI technology. First = vacuum tubes, second = transistors, third = ICs."],
["Which of the following is generally associated with the fifth generation of computers?",["Vacuum tubes","Transistors","Artificial intelligence and parallel processing","Mechanical gears"],2,"Correct: artificial intelligence and parallel processing. Fifth-generation development focuses on intelligent systems, natural-language processing, expert systems, robotics and advanced parallel processing. The other options belong to earlier eras."],
["The primary objective of fifth-generation computer development has been to:",["Replace ICs with vacuum tubes","Develop machines capable of intelligent decision-making and natural-language interaction","Eliminate memory from computers","Increase dependence on punched cards"],1,"Correct: intelligent decision-making and natural-language interaction. Fifth-generation concepts emphasize AI, knowledge processing, language understanding, robotics and parallel processing rather than older hardware technologies."],
["Consider the following statements: 1. First-generation computers used vacuum tubes. 2. Second-generation computers used transistors. 3. Third-generation computers used integrated circuits. 4. Fourth-generation computers used microprocessors. Which statements are correct?",["Only 1 and 2","Only 2 and 3","1, 2, 3 and 4","Only 1, 3 and 4"],2,"Correct: all four. The standard exam sequence is: 1st—vacuum tubes; 2nd—transistors; 3rd—integrated circuits; 4th—microprocessors/VLSI. This is one of the most important computer-awareness sequences."],
["Arrange the following technologies in chronological order: 1. Microprocessor 2. Vacuum tube 3. Integrated circuit 4. Transistor",["2 → 4 → 3 → 1","4 → 2 → 3 → 1","2 → 3 → 4 → 1","3 → 4 → 2 → 1"],0,"Correct: vacuum tube → transistor → IC → microprocessor. These map to the first four computer generations respectively. Memorize the shortcut: Vacuum → Transistor → IC → Microprocessor."],
["Which pair is incorrectly matched?",["First Generation — Vacuum Tubes","Second Generation — Transistors","Third Generation — Integrated Circuits","Fourth Generation — Vacuum Tubes"],3,"Correct: Fourth Generation — Vacuum Tubes is incorrect. Fourth-generation computers are associated with microprocessors and VLSI. Vacuum tubes belong to the first generation."],
["Which of the following correctly distinguishes second- and third-generation computers?",["Second used ICs; third used vacuum tubes","Second used transistors; third used integrated circuits","Second used microprocessors; third used transistors","Both primarily used vacuum tubes"],1,"Correct: second generation used transistors, third generation used integrated circuits. This distinction is frequently tested in banking and other competitive exams."],
["Which one of the following combinations is correctly matched?",["ENIAC — Transistor-based computer","UNIVAC I — First-generation computer","IBM PC — First-generation computer","Intel 4004 — Vacuum-tube processor"],1,"Correct: UNIVAC I — First-generation computer. UNIVAC I used vacuum-tube technology and was an early commercial computer. ENIAC was also vacuum-tube based, IBM PC belongs to the microprocessor era, and Intel 4004 is a microprocessor."],
["Which of the following statements about the evolution of computer technology is NOT correct?",["Vacuum tubes were replaced by transistors.","Transistors were followed by integrated circuits.","Integrated circuits contributed to smaller and more reliable computers.","Microprocessors increased the physical size of computers significantly."],3,"Correct: microprocessors increased physical size is NOT correct. Microprocessors and high integration enabled miniaturization, lower cost and personal computers. The other three statements correctly describe technological evolution."],
["A computer uses VLSI technology, a microprocessor, semiconductor memory and is designed for personal use. It would most appropriately be classified as belonging to which generation?",["First","Second","Third","Fourth"],3,"Correct: Fourth generation. Microprocessors, VLSI, semiconductor memory and personal computers are hallmark developments of the fourth generation."],
["Which of the following pairs represents the correct chronological progression of major computing technologies?",["Transistor → Vacuum Tube → IC → Microprocessor","Vacuum Tube → Transistor → IC → Microprocessor","IC → Vacuum Tube → Transistor → Microprocessor","Vacuum Tube → IC → Transistor → Microprocessor"],1,"Correct: Vacuum Tube → Transistor → IC → Microprocessor. This is the standard technological progression from first through fourth generation."]
];

const DETAILED_NOTES = ["Correct: Charles Babbage — widely called the Father of the Computer. He designed the Difference Engine and Analytical Engine; the Analytical Engine had concepts resembling memory, processing, input and output.\n\nA) John von Neumann — associated with the stored-program architecture. Data and instructions are stored in memory, with ALU, Control Unit, memory and I/O as major components.\n\nC) Herman Hollerith — developed punched-card tabulation, famously used for the 1890 US Census. His tabulating business is part of the corporate history that led toward IBM.\n\nD) Alan Turing — pioneer of theoretical computer science. Turing Machine and Turing Test are key exam associations.\n\nExam memory: Babbage = Analytical Engine; von Neumann = stored program; Hollerith = punched cards; Turing = Turing Machine/Test.","Correct: Analytical Engine — designed by Charles Babbage as a general-purpose mechanical computing concept. It had a \"Store\" for memory, a \"Mill\" for processing and mechanisms for input/output.\n\nA) ENIAC — early electronic general-purpose computer using vacuum tubes; associated with Mauchly and Eckert.\n\nB) UNIVAC I — one of the earliest commercial computers; vacuum-tube, first-generation era.\n\nD) Mark I — Harvard Mark I, an electromechanical computer associated with Howard Aiken.\n\nExam memory: Babbage → Analytical Engine; Mauchly/Eckert → ENIAC; Aiken → Mark I.","Correct: Difference Engine — Babbage designed it to automate calculation of mathematical tables using the method of finite differences. It was mechanical, not an electronic stored-program computer.\n\nA) Logical operations — modern electronic computers perform these through logic circuits/ALU; this was not the main purpose of the Difference Engine.\n\nC) Electronic program storage — belongs to later electronic stored-program computers.\n\nD) Punched-card processing — punched cards are strongly associated with Jacquard and Hollerith, not the Difference Engine.\n\nExam memory: Difference Engine = mathematical tables; Analytical Engine = general-purpose concept.","Correct: Charles Babbage — designed both the Difference Engine and Analytical Engine. The Analytical Engine anticipated a processor, memory, input and output system.\n\nB) Howard Aiken — associated with Harvard Mark I.\n\nC) John Mauchly — major developer of ENIAC with J. Presper Eckert.\n\nD) J. Presper Eckert — co-developer of ENIAC and other early electronic computers.\n\nExam memory: Babbage = Analytical Engine; Aiken = Mark I; Mauchly + Eckert = ENIAC.","Correct: Ada Lovelace — wrote the famous algorithm for Babbage's Analytical Engine intended to calculate Bernoulli numbers and is widely regarded as the first computer programmer.\n\nA) First electronic computer — not Ada; ENIAC is an early electronic general-purpose computer.\n\nC) First transistor — developed at Bell Labs by John Bardeen, Walter Brattain and William Shockley.\n\nD) First microprocessor — Intel 4004 is the key commercial milestone; Federico Faggin, Ted Hoff, Stanley Mazor and Masatoshi Shima are important names.\n\nExam memory: Ada → first programmer; Babbage → Analytical Engine.","Correct: Herman Hollerith — developed a punched-card tabulating machine for large-scale data processing.\n\nB) Blaise Pascal — developed Pascaline, a mechanical calculator.\n\nC) Joseph Jacquard — introduced punched-card control for patterns in the Jacquard loom; this is an important precursor to punched-card information processing.\n\nD) Konrad Zuse — German computing pioneer; Z3 is a famous programmable electromechanical computer.\n\nExam memory: Hollerith = census/tabulation; Jacquard = punched-card loom; Zuse = Z3.","Correct: 1890 US Census — Hollerith's punched-card system was used to process census data and greatly reduced tabulation time.\n\nA/C/D) These dates are distractors. The exam association to memorize is specifically 1890 US Census → Hollerith.\n\nRelated fact: Hollerith's system represented data using punched holes and electrically sensed them, an important step toward automated data processing.\n\nExam memory: 1890 US Census + punched cards = Hollerith.","Correct: Blaise Pascal — developed Pascaline, one of the earliest mechanical calculating machines.\n\nB) Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz — developed the Stepped Reckoner and advanced mechanical calculation.\n\nC) Charles Babbage — Difference Engine and Analytical Engine.\n\nD) George Boole — Boolean algebra, which became fundamental to digital logic and computer circuits.\n\nExam memory: Pascal → Pascaline; Leibniz → Stepped Reckoner; Babbage → Engines; Boole → Boolean algebra.","Correct: Mechanical calculating machine — Pascaline used mechanical wheels/gears for arithmetic operations.\n\nIt was not an electronic computer, did not use vacuum tubes and was not a modern programmable stored-program machine.\n\nRelated knowledge: Mechanical calculators were an important bridge between manual arithmetic and later automated computing. Pascaline is therefore important in the history of calculating devices even though it predates electronic computers by centuries.\n\nExam memory: Pascaline = mechanical calculator.","Correct: Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz — developed the Stepped Reckoner, a mechanical calculator capable of addition, subtraction, multiplication and division.\n\nA) Joseph Jacquard — punched-card loom.\n\nC) Herman Hollerith — punched-card tabulation.\n\nD) Howard Aiken — Harvard Mark I.\n\nRelated fact: Leibniz also developed binary arithmetic ideas, which are historically important because modern digital computers use binary representation.\n\nExam memory: Leibniz → Stepped Reckoner + binary mathematics.","Correct: Vacuum tubes — the defining electronic technology of first-generation computers.\n\nA) Transistors → second generation.\nB) Integrated circuits → third generation.\nD) Microprocessors → fourth generation.\n\nVacuum tubes were large, consumed significant power, generated heat and were less reliable than later semiconductor devices.\n\nExam memory: 1st = Vacuum Tube; 2nd = Transistor; 3rd = IC; 4th = Microprocessor.","Correct: Machine language — first-generation computers were programmed primarily in machine-level binary instructions.\n\nA) Small size/low heat — opposite of first-generation characteristics; vacuum tubes made machines large and hot.\n\nC) VLSI — associated mainly with fourth-generation systems.\n\nD) GUI — graphical user interfaces became common much later with personal-computer operating systems.\n\nExam memory: First generation = vacuum tubes + machine language + huge size + high heat.","Correct: ENIAC — an early first-generation electronic general-purpose computer using vacuum tubes.\n\nB) IBM PC — belongs to the microprocessor/personal-computer era.\n\nC) Apple Macintosh — introduced in the personal-computer era and used microprocessor technology.\n\nD) Intel 4004 — a microprocessor, introduced in 1971; it is a fourth-generation technology milestone rather than a first-generation computer.\n\nExam memory: ENIAC = first generation; Intel 4004 = microprocessor.","Correct: Electronic Numerical Integrator and Computer.\n\nENIAC was an early electronic general-purpose computer developed by John Mauchly and J. Presper Eckert. It used thousands of vacuum tubes and was built for high-speed numerical calculations.\n\nExam traps: do not confuse ENIAC with EDVAC (Electronic Discrete Variable Automatic Computer) or UNIVAC (Universal Automatic Computer).\n\nExam memory: ENIAC = Electronic Numerical Integrator and Computer.","Correct: John Mauchly and J. Presper Eckert — key developers of ENIAC.\n\nA) Babbage + Ada → Analytical Engine/early programming history.\n\nC) Howard Aiken → Harvard Mark I; Grace Hopper → compiler/COBOL pioneer.\n\nD) John von Neumann → stored-program architecture; Alan Turing → theoretical computer science/Turing Machine.\n\nRelated fact: Eckert and Mauchly also worked on EDVAC and later UNIVAC-related developments.\n\nExam memory: Mauchly + Eckert = ENIAC.","Correct: Large numbers of vacuum tubes and enormous power consumption — a major limitation of ENIAC and first-generation systems.\n\nVacuum tubes generated heat, occupied substantial space and could fail, creating maintenance problems.\n\nA) Transistors — ENIAC did not use them.\nB) No arithmetic capability — false; ENIAC was designed for complex numerical calculations.\nD) Could not calculate — false; calculation was its primary purpose.\n\nExam memory: Vacuum tubes → size + heat + power + maintenance.","Correct: Stored-program architecture — EDVAC is historically important for the idea of storing instructions in memory along with data.\n\nA) Punched-card processing — important earlier in data processing.\nC) Cloud computing — modern distributed computing model.\nD) Artificial intelligence — much later field of computing.\n\nRelated fact: Stored programs allowed computers to change tasks by changing instructions in memory rather than physically rewiring the machine.\n\nExam memory: EDVAC → stored program; von Neumann → architecture associated with it.","Correct: John von Neumann — strongly associated with the stored-program computer architecture.\n\nA key idea is that instructions and data can reside in the same memory. Major functional units include ALU, Control Unit, Memory, Input and Output.\n\nB) Hollerith → punched-card tabulation.\nC) Pascal → Pascaline.\nD) Jacquard → punched-card loom control.\n\nExam memory: “stored program” or “von Neumann architecture” → John von Neumann.","Correct: Second generation — transistors replaced vacuum tubes.\n\nA) First → vacuum tubes.\nC) Third → integrated circuits.\nD) Fourth → microprocessors/VLSI.\n\nTransistors made computers smaller, faster, more reliable and less power-hungry than vacuum-tube machines.\n\nExam memory: Generation sequence = Vacuum → Transistor → IC → Microprocessor.","Correct: Transistors and assembly language — characteristic of second-generation computers.\n\nSecond-generation systems moved away from vacuum tubes and saw wider use of assembly and high-level languages.\n\nA) Machine language only → strongly associated with first-generation programming.\nC) IC + VLSI → later generations, especially third/fourth.\nD) Microprocessors → fourth generation.\n\nRelated fact: FORTRAN and COBOL became important during the transition to higher-level programming.\n\nExam memory: 2nd generation = transistor.","Correct: Lower heat generation and greater reliability.\n\nCompared with vacuum tubes, transistors are smaller, consume less power, generate less heat, switch faster and are more durable.\n\nA) Higher power → opposite.\nB) Larger size → opposite.\nD) Slower speed → opposite in the historical comparison.\n\nRelated fact: The transistor was developed at Bell Labs by John Bardeen, Walter Brattain and William Shockley.\n\nExam memory: Transistor = smaller + cooler + faster + reliable.","Correct: Third generation — Integrated Circuits (ICs).\n\nAn IC combines multiple electronic components on a semiconductor chip, reducing size and improving reliability and cost.\n\nA) First → vacuum tubes.\nB) Second → transistors.\nD) Fourth → microprocessors/VLSI.\n\nRelated fact: IC technology later progressed through SSI, MSI, LSI and VLSI levels of integration.\n\nExam memory: Third generation = IC.","Correct: Integrated circuits — they replaced many discrete components by integrating electronic components onto semiconductor chips.\n\nA) Vacuum tubes → first generation.\nC) Magnetic drums → older memory technology, not the defining replacement here.\nD) Mechanical gears → mechanical calculators/early machines.\n\nRelated fact: Increasing integration is what eventually enabled microprocessors and modern compact electronics.\n\nExam memory: IC = many components integrated on a chip.","Correct: FORTRAN and COBOL.\n\nFORTRAN = Formula Translation; developed at IBM and strongly associated with scientific and numerical computation.\n\nCOBOL = Common Business-Oriented Language; designed for business/data-processing applications and remains historically important in banking and enterprise systems.\n\nB) Machine language → first-generation context.\nC) Assembly only → second-generation context.\nD) HTML/CSS → web technologies, not third-generation computer-generation languages.\n\nExam memory: FORTRAN → scientific; COBOL → business.","Correct: Grace Hopper — compiler pioneer and strongly associated with COBOL.\n\nShe worked on early compiler technology and helped make programming more accessible through higher-level languages.\n\nB) Ada Lovelace → first programmer/Analytical Engine.\nC) Kathleen Booth → important early work on assembly language.\nD) Radia Perlman → networking pioneer, especially Spanning Tree Protocol.\n\nRelated fact: The famous moth “debugging” story is associated with Hopper, although the word debug predates the incident.\n\nExam memory: Grace Hopper → compiler + COBOL.","Correct: Fourth generation — microprocessors became the defining technology.\n\nA) First → vacuum tubes.\nB) Second → transistors.\nC) Third → ICs.\n\nFourth-generation computers used microprocessors, VLSI and semiconductor memory, making personal computers practical and affordable.\n\nExam memory: 4th generation = microprocessor + VLSI + PC.","Correct: Microprocessor — Intel 4004, introduced in 1971, is widely regarded as the first commercially available microprocessor.\n\nIt was a 4-bit processor and an important milestone in putting CPU functions onto a single chip.\n\nRelated names: Federico Faggin, Ted Hoff, Stanley Mazor and Masatoshi Shima were important contributors.\n\nA/C/D are system categories, not what the 4004 itself was.\n\nExam memory: Intel 4004 → 1971 → 4-bit microprocessor.","Correct: Integrated-circuit technology — increasing semiconductor integration made microprocessors possible.\n\nThe progression was from individual components to ICs and then increasingly dense integration such as LSI and VLSI. This allowed many processing functions to fit onto a chip.\n\nA) Mechanical engineering → important historically but not the key enabling technology.\nC) Vacuum tubes → first generation.\nD) Punched cards → data input/control technology, not the basis of microprocessors.\n\nExam memory: IC advancement → LSI/VLSI → microprocessor.","Correct: Very Large Scale Integration.\n\nSSI = Small Scale Integration.\nMSI = Medium Scale Integration.\nLSI = Large Scale Integration.\nVLSI = Very Large Scale Integration.\nULSI = Ultra Large Scale Integration.\n\nVLSI means a very large number of electronic components/transistors can be integrated on a semiconductor chip and is strongly associated with fourth-generation computers.\n\nExam memory: SSI → MSI → LSI → VLSI → ULSI.","Correct: Fourth generation — VLSI and microprocessor technology are major identifiers.\n\nA) First → vacuum tubes.\nB) Second → transistors.\nC) Third → ICs.\n\nRelated fact: VLSI enabled much greater miniaturization and helped drive the personal-computer revolution.\n\nExam memory: If the question says “microprocessor + VLSI”, choose fourth generation.","Correct: Artificial intelligence and parallel processing — commonly associated with fifth-generation computing.\n\nFifth-generation goals include AI, natural-language processing, expert systems, knowledge-based systems, robotics and advanced parallel processing.\n\nA) Vacuum tubes → first.\nB) Transistors → second.\nD) Mechanical gears → early mechanical computing.\n\nImportant nuance: Fifth generation is mainly a broad development goal/capability category rather than one single hardware component.\n\nExam memory: Fifth generation → AI/NLP/robotics/parallel processing.","Correct: Intelligent decision-making and natural-language interaction.\n\nThe fifth-generation vision focused on computers that could work with knowledge, understand human language, reason and assist in intelligent tasks.\n\nA) Vacuum tubes → obsolete first-generation technology.\nC) Eliminating memory → incorrect; intelligent systems require substantial memory/knowledge processing.\nD) Punched cards → older input/data-processing technology.\n\nExam memory: Fifth generation = intelligence + language + knowledge processing.","Correct: All four statements are correct.\n\n1st → Vacuum tubes: huge, hot, power-hungry.\n2nd → Transistors: smaller, faster, more reliable.\n3rd → ICs: multiple components integrated on chips.\n4th → Microprocessors/VLSI: personal-computer era.\n\nThis is a core banking-exam sequence. If an option changes even one generation, it is usually the incorrect statement.\n\nExam memory: 1-Vacuum, 2-Transistor, 3-IC, 4-Microprocessor.","Correct order: Vacuum tube → Transistor → Integrated Circuit → Microprocessor.\n\nThis is the technological progression behind the first four generations.\n\nVacuum tubes were replaced by transistors; transistors and other components were increasingly integrated into ICs; higher integration eventually produced microprocessors.\n\nExam memory: V → T → IC → μP, or simply 1 → 2 → 3 → 4.","Correct: Fourth Generation — Vacuum Tubes is the incorrect pair.\n\nFirst generation → vacuum tubes.\nSecond → transistors.\nThird → ICs.\nFourth → microprocessors/VLSI.\n\nRelated fact: Vacuum tubes were active electronic devices used for amplification/switching but were bulky and power-hungry. Semiconductor transistors solved many of these limitations.\n\nExam memory: Never pair vacuum tube with fourth generation.","Correct: Second generation used transistors; third generation used integrated circuits.\n\nA transistor is a semiconductor switching/amplifying device. An IC can contain many electronic components, including transistors, on a single chip.\n\nA) reverses the generations.\nC) reverses the generations and puts microprocessors too early.\nD) ignores the major technological transition.\n\nExam memory: 2nd = transistor; 3rd = IC.","Correct: UNIVAC I — an early commercial computer and a first-generation vacuum-tube system.\n\nA) ENIAC — also first-generation and vacuum-tube based, so “transistor-based” is wrong.\nC) IBM PC — personal computer of the microprocessor era, not first generation.\nD) Intel 4004 — a microprocessor, not a vacuum-tube processor.\n\nRelated fact: UNIVAC stands for Universal Automatic Computer and is an important milestone in commercial computing.\n\nExam memory: UNIVAC I → early commercial computer → first generation.","Correct: “Microprocessors increased the physical size of computers significantly” is NOT correct.\n\nMicroprocessors and higher levels of integration actually enabled miniaturization, lower cost and the spread of personal computers.\n\nA) Vacuum tubes → replaced by transistors.\nB) Transistors → followed by increasing IC integration.\nC) ICs → improved reliability and reduced size/cost.\n\nExam memory: More integration = smaller, cheaper, more powerful computers.","Correct: Fourth generation.\n\nThe clues are VLSI + microprocessor + semiconductor memory + personal use. Together these strongly identify fourth-generation personal-computer technology.\n\nFirst → vacuum tubes.\nSecond → transistors.\nThird → ICs.\nFourth → microprocessors/VLSI.\n\nRelated fact: Semiconductor memory uses semiconductor devices for storing information and became central to modern computer systems.\n\nExam memory: Microprocessor + VLSI + PC = fourth generation.","Correct progression: Vacuum Tube → Transistor → IC → Microprocessor.\n\nVacuum tubes define first generation; transistors define second; integrated circuits define third; microprocessors/VLSI define fourth.\n\nA/C/D put these technologies in the wrong historical order.\n\nRelated fact: Fifth-generation discussions then move toward AI, natural-language processing, expert systems and parallel processing.\n\nExam memory: VT → Transistor → IC → Microprocessor."];

